WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.970
In week six of a programming course, a

2
00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.930
third of students say that after a equals

3
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.550
b, giving b a new value also changes a.

4
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.270
They passed every coding quiz.

5
00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:13.610
How?

6
00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.630
Today: the hidden models students hold

7
00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.670
of how programs run, the research on

8
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.070
novice misconceptions, and tracing

9
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.370
activities that fix them.

10
00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.630
A notional machine is an idealised

11
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.950
computer that explains how a language

12
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.730
runs, at the level a learner needs.

13
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.320
Students always build some mental model.

14
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.640
Our job is to make sure

15
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.820
it is a viable one.

16
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.500
Sorva's review catalogues dozens

17
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.840
of novice misconceptions.

18
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.600
Assignment is seen as a lasting link.

19
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.240
Variables are thought to hold

20
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.720
several values at once.

21
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.540
And students assume the computer

22
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.240
understands what they meant.

23
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.240
Here is a short trace.

24
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.530
Students with the link model answer seven

25
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.230
for both, and that wrong answer tells you

26
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.950
exactly which model they hold.

27
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.900
Research by Lister and

28
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.820
colleagues suggests a sequence.

29
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.610
Students who cannot trace code

30
00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.410
rarely explain it, and those who

31
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.990
cannot explain it rarely write it.

32
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.650
So assess tracing and explaining in

33
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.510
plain English, not only writing.

34
00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.570
Draw names with arrows to their values on

35
00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.750
the board for every lecture example.

36
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.130
Use visualisers such as Python Tutor.

37
00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:40.090
And use multiple-choice tracing

38
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.390
questions whose wrong options each

39
00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:43.770
match one known misconception.

40
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:45.780
Quick check!

41
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.740
b is five, then a equals b,

42
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.920
then b becomes seven.

43
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.350
What does print a, b show?

44
00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:58.820
Five and seven.

45
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.660
Assignment makes a refer to the

46
00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.300
value b had at that moment.

47
00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:07.850
Giving b a new value later rebinds only b.

48
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.140
In the app, open the trace lab.

49
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.120
Predict each variable after every

50
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.920
line, then step through and compare

51
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.400
your prediction with the machine.

52
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:20.150
Your turn.

53
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.680
A student answers seven, seven.

54
00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:25.890
Which misconception does

55
00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.790
that answer reveal?

56
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.750
It reveals the belief that

57
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.370
assignment creates a lasting link.

58
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.060
Remember: every student holds a notional

59
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.820
machine, tracing comes before writing,

60
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.520
and wrong answers are diagnostic.

61
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.870
Try it in your next lecture: start with

62
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.030
three tracing questions, each with

63
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.630
distractors that match a known

64
00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:49.970
misconception.

65
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.420
Students vote on their phones.

66
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.160
Then redraw memory for the

67
00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.920
question most students missed.
